---
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import ScreenCard from '../components/ScreenCard.astro';
import { siteData } from '../lib/site';
import { SCREENS } from '../lib/features';

const { business: b } = await siteData();
---

<Prose
  title={`Your data | ${b.brandName}`}
  description="Where OSuite keeps your books, how backups and restores work, the edit history that cannot be quietly rewritten, and exactly what leaves your PC."
  lines={['Your data,', 'your PC']}
  standfirst="Your books live on your computer, in a database of their own. Here is how they are kept safe, and the little that ever leaves."
  shot={{ src: SCREENS.backups, alt: 'The backups screen: daily schedule, a second folder for copies, and the list of backups with restore', focus: '30% 0' }}
>
  <h2>On your computer, not ours</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop edition keeps your company’s data in a PostgreSQL database that OSuite installs and runs on your own PC. Invoices, ledgers,
    stock and customers never pass through our servers. Daily work needs no internet connection at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Backups you do not have to remember</h2>
  <p>
    OSuite takes a backup every day on its own, keeps the most recent ones, and can copy each one to a second folder you choose: another drive,
    a USB disk, or a OneDrive or Google Drive folder. You can also take a backup at any moment and download it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A restore never touches your live data until the backup has been checked. It is restored into a separate database first, checked for
    the right version and an intact edit history, and only then swapped in. Your current data is backed up just before, so a restore can be undone.
  </p>

  <h2>An edit history that cannot be quietly rewritten</h2>
  <p>
    Every change is recorded: who made it, when, and what changed. Each entry is chained to the one before it with a SHA-256 hash, so altering or
    deleting an old entry, even directly in the database, breaks the chain and shows up when the history is checked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Posted invoices, bills and journals cannot be edited; they are corrected with a cancellation or a note, which is itself recorded. A closed
    financial year is locked against new entries.
  </p>

  <h2>People and passwords</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone has their own login, so every change carries a name. Passwords are stored only as salted scrypt hashes. Administrators manage users;
    a licence covers a set number of them.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaves your PC</h2>
  <p>
    Only what the licence needs. When you start the trial, OSuite sends the name, email, phone number and company you type in. Every 14 days it
    checks in with the licence: the licence token, a fingerprint of the computer, the computer’s name, the OSuite version and the operating system.
    Nothing from your books is ever sent.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a check-in cannot be made, you have 7 more days. After that OSuite turns read-only until it can check in again: you can still open every
    record, print and take backups. Your data is never locked away from you.
  </p>

  <h2>In the cloud</h2>
  <p>
    If you choose cloud hosting, we run OSuite for you and take care of backups, and it opens in a browser from anywhere. The software and the
    edit history are the same, and your data remains yours to download at any time.
  </p>
  <p>Questions about data and privacy: see the <a href="/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a> or <a href="/contact">write to us</a>.</p>

  <section slot="after" class="container card-row more" aria-label="Related screens">
    <ScreenCard title="Year-end closing" text="A closed financial year is locked against new entries." shot={{ src: SCREENS.closing, alt: 'Year-end closing locks a financial year' }} i={0} />
    <ScreenCard title="The day book" text="Every posted entry, in order, with who posted it." shot={{ src: SCREENS.dayBook, alt: 'The day book of posted entries' }} i={1} />
    <ScreenCard title="Trial balance" text="Checks that the books agree, at any date you pick." shot={{ src: SCREENS.trialBalance, alt: 'The trial balance' }} i={2} />
  </section>
</Prose>

<style>
  .more { padding-bottom: var(--section); }
</style>
